Done — only `impl/d1_race.c` (via the `impl_2` working copy) and `strategies/d1_race.md` were touched.

Summary of the round: d1_race is the router/library layer, and it was already tracking its best gated sibling within noise, so I made three mechanism improvements rather than a redesign. First, dispatch is flattened: `fft1d_execute`/`fft1d_chain` are now each a single branch-free indirect call from the plan's first cache line, so the shipped path is exactly the bare arm path the race timed (measured identical to a statically-linked d1_prime at L=13; the old forwarding's apparent 1ns "win" over the bare arm turned out to be binary layout luck, documented honestly). Second, I adopted gen_r12's variant-lane mechanism: the arm roster is now a table allowing extra `-D` flags per lane, with the flag string mixed into the wisdom hash, and the first lane is `d1_composite -DUSE_ZMM4` — the cross-host A/B their r1 record explicitly left open; it gates and races cleanly (base ymm2 won on wallaby as they predicted; the scoring node decides for real). Third, the scalar self engine now sits out the exe race at big cells (it ate ~3s of the 5s budget at L=100003 while never winning), which cut the L=65537 B=16 plan from 4.5s to 1.9s and improved the shipped min from 856 to 779µs; salt bumped exe.r1→exe.r2. All graded cells re-verified on wallaby: check.py PASS everywhere (worst rel L2 1.4e-15 vs tol 1e-12), map-chain gates PASS, two-process repeatability identical. The Ice Lake reservation was down again, so these are wallaby numbers; the scoring node re-races everything fresh since every sibling hash changed.

Ended on: flattened raced-path dispatch + gen_r12 variant-knob lanes (first lane d1_composite+zmm4) + self-exclusion from big-cell races; best wallaby: B=1 0.010–0.016µs at L=13 (routed d1_prime), batched 0.009µs/xform at L=13 B=512; rel L2 1.4e-16–1.4e-15 (tol 1e-12), all cells PASS.
